{"id":"erase-a-node-without-the-head","lc":"delete-node-in-a-linked-list","title":"Erase a Node Without the Head","diff":"medium","pattern":"linked-list","tags":["linked-list","in-place"],"statement":"<p>A singly linked list has distinct values. You are handed <b>one node</b> of it, <code>node</code>, but not the head. The node is never the last one in the list.</p>\n<p>Make the list look as if <code>node</code> had been deleted: its value must disappear, every other value must stay in its original order, and the list must get one node shorter. Change the list in place and return nothing.</p>\n<p>From <code>node</code> you can only see the nodes after it, so the tests show just that part: the list starting at <code>node</code>. The expected output is that same part after your change, read from the same node object. The nodes before it are untouched.</p>","examples":[{"in":"node = [7, 3, 9]","out":"[3, 9]","why":"You cannot reach the node before 7, so you cannot unlink 7 itself. Copy 3 into this node, then unlink the node that held 3."},{"in":"node = [4, 1]","out":"[1]","why":"The node right before the tail. After copying 1 forward, the old tail is unlinked and this node becomes the tail."}],"constraints":["2 ≤ nodes from node to the end ≤ 1000","-1000 ≤ Node.val ≤ 1000","All values in the list are distinct","node is not the tail"],"fn":{"name":"eraseNode","params":[["node","ListNode"]],"returns":"void","mutates":0},"tests":[{"args":[[7,3,9]],"expected":[3,9]},{"args":[[4,1]],"expected":[1]},{"args":[[1,2,3,4,5]],"expected":[2,3,4,5]},{"args":[[0,-1]],"expected":[-1]},{"args":[[-1000,1000]],"expected":[1000]},{"args":[[5,4,3,2,1]],"expected":[4,3,2,1]},{"args":[[10,20,30]],"expected":[20,30]},{"args":[[2,9,6,1]],"expected":[9,6,1]},{"args":[[-500,-499,-498,-497,-496,-495,-494,-493,-492,-491,-490,-489,-488,-487,-486,-485,-484,-483,-482,-481,-480,-479,-478,-477,-476,-475,-474,-473,-472,-471,-470,-469,-468,-467,-466,-465,-464,-463,-462,-461,-460,-459,-458,-457,-456,-455