414,415,416,417,418,419,420,421,422,423,424,425,426,427,428,429,430,431,432,433,434,435,436,437,438,439,440,441,442,443,444,445,446,447,448,449,450,451,452,453,454,455,456,457,458,459,460,461,462,463,464,465,466,467,468,469,470,471,472,473,474,475,476,477,478,479,480,481,482,483,484,485,486,487,488,489,490,491,492,493,494,495,496,497,498,499]},{"args":[[113,-536]],"expected":[-536]},{"args":[[-203,-527]],"expected":[-527]},{"args":[[102,-426,461,201,-566,132,-263]],"expected":[-426,461,201,-566,132,-263]},{"args":[[-390,-617,-821,950,575,-171,-753]],"expected":[-617,-821,950,575,-171,-753]},{"args":[[174,301,-393,-769,-426,959,264,8]],"expected":[301,-393,-769,-426,959,264,8]},{"args":[[-178,-542]],"expected":[-542]},{"args":[[-656,-344,-646,-590,-451,145,-57,778]],"expected":[-344,-646,-590,-451,145,-57,778]},{"args":[[-129,-834,958,202,-992,-526]],"expected":[-834,958,202,-992,-526]},{"args":[[650,196,-902]],"expected":[196,-902]},{"args":[[-455,-344]],"expected":[-344]}],"gen":"(r, big) => [r.distinct(big ? 1000 : r.int(2, 8), -1000, 1000)]","hints":["Normally you delete a node by pointing the previous node past it. Here there is no way back to the previous node. Which node can you delete?","You can easily delete the node after this one. What would you need to do first so that deleting it looks like deleting this one?","Copy the next node's value into this node, then point this node's next past the next node."],"explain":{"intuition":"<p>Deleting a node from a singly linked list means changing the <code>next</code> pointer of the node before it. You do not have that node, and you cannot walk backwards. So you cannot remove <i>this</i> node object.</p>\n<p>But nobody outside sees node objects, only the values in order. So change the question: make this node <b>become</b> its successor by copying the next value in, then remove the successor, which you can reach. The list now reads exactly as if the original node were gone. That is also why the node must not be the tail: there is no successor to copy from.</p>","steps":["Copy <code>node.next.val</code> into <code>node.val</code>.","Set <code>node.next = node.next.next</code>, skipping the old successor.","Done: the list is one node shorter and the old value is gone."],"pitfalls":["Setting <code>node = node.next</code>. That only rebinds a local variable; the list does not change.","Shifting every value left one step and then failing to cut the last node, which leaves the final value twice.","Using this trick in real code where other parts hold references to nodes. The object you meant to delete is still in the list, and a reference to the successor now points at a detached node."],"complexity":{"time":"O(1)","space":"O(1)"}},"solutions":[{"title":"Shift every value left","idea":"Copy each next value one step back along the rest of the list, then cut off the last node.","time":"O(n)","space":"O(1)","ts":"function eraseNode(node: ListNode | null): void {\n  let cur = node!;\n  while (cur.next!.next) {\n    cur.val = cur.next!.val;\n    cur = cur.next!;\n  }\n  cur.val = cur.next!.val;\n  cur.next = null; // drop the old tail\n}","py":"class Solution:\n    def eraseNode(self, node: Optional[ListNode]) -> None:\n        cur = node\n        while cur.next.next:\n            cur.val = cur.next.val\n            cur = cur.next\n        cur.val = cur.next.val\n        cur.next = None  # drop the old tail","js":"function eraseNode(node) {\n  let cur = node;\n  while (cur.next.next) {\n    cur.val = cur.next.val;\n    cur = cur.next;\n  }\n  cur.val = cur.next.val;\n  cur.next = null;\n}"},{"title":"Copy the next value, skip the next node","idea":"Turn this node into its successor, then unlink the successor. Only two nodes are touched.","time":"O(1)","space":"O(1)","ts":"function eraseNode(node: ListNode | null): void {\n  node!.val = node!.next!.val;\n  node!.next = node!.next!.next;\n}","py":"class Solution:\n    def eraseNode(self, node: Optional[ListNode]) -> None:\n        node.val = node.next.val\n        node.next = node.next.next","js":"function eraseNode(node) {\n  node.val = node.next.val;\n  node.next = node.next.next;\n}"}],"followups":["Why can this trick not delete the last node, and what would you need to change in the list design to allow it?","Delete every node with a given value when you do have the head. How does a dummy node help?"],"related":[],"kind":"function","nEx":2,"nBig":2,"lcTitle":"Delete Node in a Linked List","acc":0.008451680220533296,"co":[["Adobe",82,0],["Apple",51,0],["Microsoft",46,180],["Bloomberg",43,180],["Oracle",41,0],["Amazon",38,0],["Meta",38,90],["Google",35,90],["Ola Cabs",0,30]]}